00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.590
So now that we have entered the total
sales, total income and the average sales

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.970
data, we now need to go back to the Word
document to see what else is required.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:17.220
And if we look on there, we are being
asked to show if the target sales of 250

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.159
for each product were met or not so that
they can look at adjusting our offers.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.400
So this will require an IF statement and
an IF statement is quite straight forward.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:27.750
It is a logical argument.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:33.300
If something is equal to or greater than
or less than, it will simply display

00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:38.169
a value of true, false or specific
element of text that you wish to display.

00:00:38.989 --> 00:00:42.040
So, what we need to do first is
I'm going to give this column a new

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:50.880
heading, and it's going to be target
met and what we need to do then is

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:55.890
we need to decide in this column,
whether the target of 250 was met.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.930
Now, the total sales is
indicated in column J.

00:01:00.269 --> 00:01:03.210
So we need to reference that cell.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:09.090
And depending on what that value
is, display either yes or no.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.060
So to do that, we need to
insert an IF statement.

00:01:12.210 --> 00:01:14.370
So to do that, I've clicked on that cell.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:20.700
And if we go to the formulas and then
select insert function, the insert

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:24.899
function, dialog box comes up and it
gives you a range of the most common

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:27.410
or most recently used formulas.

00:01:27.429 --> 00:01:31.229
So we have SUMIF, subtotal, sum
average, IF, hyperlink, count.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:34.560
So what we're looking
for is the IF statement.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:38.960
So if I click on that and
then select OK it then brings

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.770
up the IF function argument.

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:46.620
So the logical test for this one
is that we need to say that the

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.590
sales are greater than 250 or not.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:55.170
So the logical argument is that,
is that cell there, which is

00:01:55.170 --> 00:02:00.330
J2, is that greater than 250.

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:03.030
So I would need to use
the greater than symbol.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:07.290
And then the number 250.

00:02:07.559 --> 00:02:12.420
So if that is greater than 250, what
do I want it to say if it's true?

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.030
And that is quite simply "yes".

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:18.440
And if the value is false, "no".

00:02:19.079 --> 00:02:22.680
So if I now select OK
that will enter that.

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:25.440
And as you can see, it is yes.

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:29.920
And you can look at that and say,
yeah, well, 378, greater than 250.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:30.840
So it should be, yes.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:36.210
This is important in relation to the
fact that you may have an awful lot

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.620
of data, you could have 500 entries
and quite simply rather than having to

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:45.420
look at each of them, you could simply
filter out for the yes and the nos.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:52.030
So again, as mentioned in the previous
videos, I can just copy that formula

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.460
down by clicking in the bottom corner.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:58.090
And when it changes to a black
crosshair, just moving it down.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.645
And as you can see there, there
were two items that did sell

00:03:03.645 --> 00:03:06.075
over 250 and three that didn't.

00:03:06.375 --> 00:03:08.475
Obviously you can just look
here and see that for yourself.

00:03:08.475 --> 00:03:13.065
But as I say, if you had a large
amount of data or this was needed to

00:03:13.065 --> 00:03:17.925
reference something else, it would be
quite complicated, so this simplifies

00:03:17.925 --> 00:03:22.425
the process and allows you to see that
whether the target was met or not.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.390
There are occasions where you may
need to use the SUMIF function

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:28.980
rather than just the IF function.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.660
And that would be when you want to
calculate a certain number of cells

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:38.550
or certain cells that meet certain
criteria and then display certain

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.870
information in relation to that.

00:03:40.725 --> 00:03:43.995
I have provided further detail on
the webpage with a link that you can

00:03:43.995 --> 00:03:47.715
follow to look at the SUMIF function
in greater detail, but for the

00:03:47.715 --> 00:03:51.405
purposes of this one, the IF statement
is what is required so that you can

00:03:51.405 --> 00:03:54.555
confirm a simple yes or no argument.

